This is a simple test with a set of fake well logs that produces a
single reflection coeff. of about R=0.1 at 8000ft or 1.43 secs. The
synthetic seismic is then a perfect snapshot of the wavelet if there
is no noise.  With a perfect seismic data set, the wavelet extractor
ought to be able to detect the wavelet length exactly. With noisy
seismic, the recovery will be less exact.

NB. the suswapbytes pipes below are required if you use SU on a little-endian machine (e.g.
Linux on intels).

To make a synthetic minicube with 5% noise
(1) Edit WaveletExtraction.xml and make a wide time gate minicube (e.g. 1-2 secs), e.g. ...

<extraction_info>
    <extraction_window_start_time>1.0</extraction_window_start_time>
    <extraction_window_end_time>2.0</extraction_window_end_time>
</extraction_info>	


(2) Generate the minicube:
% waveletExtractor WaveletExtraction.xml -v 5 --make-synthetic 40 4 .05 --dump-ML-wavelets

The minicube will then be in synth_seis.well_log.txt.stack.1.su


(3) To test the extraction:
	- Restore the WaveletExtraction.xml entry above to the desired time gate (e.g. 1.1,1.7 secs).

    - Link the synthetic minicube to the named seismic in the XML:
% ln -sf synth_seis.well_log.txt.stack.1.su stack1.su

	- Run the extractor over, e.g. all "centered" wavelets
% waveletExtractor WaveletExtraction.xml --dump-ML-parameters --dump-ML-synthetics --dump-ML-wavelets --fake-Vs -v 4 -c -NLR -N 50 | tee junk.txt

	Examine junk.txt for all sorts of useful information.
 
	- To plot the maximum likelihood wavelet:
% cat MLwavelet.MOST_LIKELY.su | suswapbytes|suxgraph

	- To plot the "tie"
% extractionViewer MLgraphing-file.MOST_LIKELY.well_log.txt 

	- To see scatterplot of wavelet span versus posterior likelihoods:
% awk '/MODE:/{print $2,$3}' junk.txt |graphing-program

	- To see progressively shorter ML wavelets:

% cat MLwavelet.mode.* | suswapbytes |suxwigb

	- To plot superposed true and synthetic seismic:

% cat MLsynth_plus_true_seis.mode.0.well_log.txt.stack.1.su |suswapbytes| suwind key=mark max=1 | suxgraph 

	- To show wavelet realisations from the posterior

% cat wavelet_realisations.su | suswapbytes | suxgraph

(4) Example of using phase constraints.

-	Alter the checkshot so the time sample at 8000ft is, say, 10ms off. Perform the
	usual centered wavelet extraction

% waveletExtractor WaveletExtraction.xml --dump-ML-parameters --dump-ML-synthetics --dump-ML-wavelets --fake-Vs -v 4 -c -NLR 
% extractionViewer MLgraphing-file.MOST_LIKELY.well_log.txt 

	- Notice the wavelet is displaced in time. It's relatively probable to have an offset wavelet shape, 
	since nothing in the prior enforces wavelet symmetry.
	- Now repeat the extraction, insisting agressively on a zero-phase wavelet:

% waveletExtractor WaveletExtraction.xml --dump-ML-parameters --dump-ML-synthetics --dump-ML-wavelets --fake-Vs -v 4 -c -NLR --constrain-phase 0 1
% extractionViewer MLgraphing-file.MOST_LIKELY.well_log.txt 

	- Note the checkshot point has now been moved about 10ms to restore the zero-phase (symmetric) character
	of the wavelet: check out time_to_depth_knot_5_well_0 in this file:

% cat MLparameters.MOST_LIKELY.txt 
